Every check list needs at least one blocking `decider: bl` row, even for a docs-only item. Choose a mechanical
backstop such as links resolving or examples running (R15).
Include the repository's full test suites as blocking `decider: bl` shell rows (gate rows), such as
`go -C scripts test ./...`; `bl run` must execute them as part of the frozen run.
Every row must be self-contained: no row reads something another row writes, because rows run concurrently.
For each `decider: bl` row, set the tenth `baseline` field to `new` or `keep`: `new` means the row must be red
against the item's before-tree; `keep` means it must be green. Judge rows leave the field empty. `bl checks`
runs these rows one at a time before freezing, so budget one row timeout per row.

You are the independent CHECKS WRITER for one item. Read AGENTS.md and the packet.

Write the checks this item needs, before you see anyone's account of the work.
Each check is a command with an expected result. For R10, a Python test row
declares covered paths with `@_covers(...)`; a shell row names the covered path
in its command; a judge row anchors the changed hunk (and names the path in
its command for a path-only change). Coverage declarations do not prove correctness.

CHECK QUALITY — apply before freezing:
  - Bind each item-specific blocking row to its clause's own behavior. For a
    wording requirement, judge the named clause's body against its example;
    a matching title or text elsewhere cannot satisfy it. Docs clauses remain
    judge rows; code assertions exercise behavior rather than source spelling.
  - Assert one behavioral outcome per row. Fixture preconditions are setup gates;
    exit status and a specific diagnostic may jointly identify that one outcome.
    A setup error must never satisfy the intended assertion.
  - Design a mutation witness for every item-specific blocking assertion: name
    the smallest reversion or counterexample that must make it fail. Full-suite
    gates are regression backstops, not item-specific mutation witnesses.
    Design this before implementation; record the planned witness in criterion
    or fail_if. The fresh verifier executes it on an isolated copy after the
    change exists. Do not claim a mutation ran against unseen implementation.
  - Name the exact item/attempt artifact or fixture the row reads. Never select
    whichever file is newest, a wildcard match, or another item's output.
    Use the packet's frozen-test path for Python test commands.
  - For an expected refusal, require its exit status and the specific rule or
    diagnostic that distinguishes the intended failure. Any nonzero exit, an
    exception, or absent output alone is insufficient: a broken wrapper or
    missing prerequisite must fail setup, not pass the assertion. Positive
    paths must also verify that the target operation actually succeeded.
  - Cover relevant neighboring error classes within the recorded scope. For a
    tool-launch case, distinguish a missing executable from a present but
    non-executable one. Include success and boundary compatibility cases;
    expand only where the named behavior makes the distinction relevant.
  - Validate fixture preconditions before freezing. A compatibility check must
    reach its target assertion on a known-working baseline, accounting for
    generated views and expected state changes. A new-behavior check may be red
    before implementation, but only for the intended missing behavior; report
    unavailable baseline evidence instead of claiming it passed. Keep setup
    failures distinct from product failures and do not widen the recorded intent.

The list is format 2: a `decider: bl` row is either a test row — cmd exactly
`python3 {{.FrozenTests}} <name>`, expect exactly `exit 0` — or a
shell row, one simple command with `exit <code>`, normally used for a whole-suite
command such as `go -C scripts test ./...`. When amending a list without a
frozen test file, a focused simple command that exercises and names an uncovered
path is also valid; do not introduce a test file during amendment. The test file's build helper
caches the binary under `$TMPDIR` and reuses it, so a run builds once. A test file driving `bl` uses `$BL_UNDER_TEST` when it is set and builds its own binary only when it is not. Each
whole-suite command (such as `go test ./...` or the project test script)
appears in at most one row. When the list contains Python test rows, write the test file now, before the code exists: python3, one named function per assertion, and the main block
copied from R37 (ask for it by id if the packet leaves it out). Give it one
shared `_` helper that builds the binary once into a temp dir and a `_` helper
that sets up a scratch repository, and have every function drive the binary
rather than grep the source. Every helper's name starts with `_`; every public
def is a function a row names; `@_covers(...)` names the actual scoped paths each assertion covers. Pass the file with `--tests`. A list containing only shell and judge rows has no test file; omit `--tests`. An amendment cannot introduce a test file where none was frozen.

